# Kit's UI, described in Kit's own notation — the other half of kit.beh.
#
# kit:self-authored
#   Read by saturation.js, which excludes this corpus from the binding-saturation
#   study. None of its other three axes is true here: this describes a UI, for
#   software that exists, and no other corpus in that study describes this app.
#   The disqualifying property is different — the app, this corpus and its
#   bindings share ONE author, written in one sitting with the bindings already
#   in mind. The measurement caught it before the argument did: every other
#   corpus scores 59–76% against the shuffled null and this one scored 10%, the
#   only one that looks like it saturates faster than chance. That is the author,
#   not the app. The study's claim is an independence claim, so this corpus
#   cannot be evidence for it — which says nothing about the corpus itself.
#
# ── Why this is a SECOND corpus and not more of kit.beh ──────────────────────
# `kit.beh` declares `# kit:no-ui` and its 0% is not a gap, it is EVIDENCE: Kit
# the command-line tool needs `runs`, `exits`, `reports`, `raises`, and the
# generator's whole vocabulary is browser verbs. Adding browser behaviours to
# that file would dilute the one measurement that makes the point.
#
# So Kit is two apps in its own registry, and only one of them has a UI.
#
# ── What this file is FOR ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# kit.beh's header states a falsifiable claim: *"this file is not a corpus that
# is waiting for bindings… the missing piece is not notation, it is a UI. That
# is the same UI James asked for in the first half of #89."*
#
# That UI now exists (kit#16 → #24 → #26 → #27 → #28). This corpus is the test
# of the claim: if it was right, a browser corpus of Kit's own surface should
# derive real Playwright, and Kit stops being a special case in its own
# registry. If it was wrong, this file says so at the same cost.
#
# ⚠️ **THE CONFOUND, STATED FIRST.** I built this UI, so a corpus I write about
# it measures recall as much as description — the same objection that made
# kit#23 use two isolated agents. It does NOT undermine the question being asked
# here, which is whether the GENERATOR can emit a runnable test for a browser
# surface, not whether two authors would pick the same nouns. That second
# question is kit#23's and is still open. Read the derived count as evidence
# about the generator, never about the notation's learnability.
#
# ── Nouns are prefixed `Kit*`, and they no longer need to be ─────────────────
# THE REASON IS GONE AS OF claude-code-bot#66 (2026-09-25). The old bindings file
# warned that THE NOUN NAMESPACE IS GLOBAL ACROSS APPS: an unprefixed `page:Home`
# here would silently reuse snip-it's binding and emit `page.goto('./')` — a test
# that RUNS, against the wrong app, with no unbound-noun warning. James ended
# that; a corpus now binds its own nouns in `kit-ui.bindings.json` beside this
# file, so the collision cannot occur.
#
# The prefixes are therefore harmless history rather than a rule, and they stay:
# renaming a noun is a change to the CORPUS and his to make (claude-code-bot#58),
# not a tidy-up. Do NOT tell the next corpus to copy the habit.
#
# Every accessible name below was READ OFF THE RUNNING APP, not invented — the
# same rule every bindings file holds itself to.
#
# ⚠️ Ids here are NOT `BEH-UI-*`, deliberately. `kit.beh` already uses that
# prefix for four behaviours of the ui.js SERVER — the loopback bind, the
# traversal refusal, the method-not-allowed handler. Same spelling, different
# subject, and one file away from each other. Ids are corpus-scoped so nothing
# would have broken; a reader would simply have been wrong.

behaviour BEH-LIST-1 "Every corpus Kit can read is listed as a project"
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitProjects
  then sees heading:KitProjectsTitle
  then sees region:KitMain
  contract a corpus that will not parse is listed AS an error, not as zero behaviours

behaviour BEH-SHEET-1 "A project shows the question sheet Kit built for it"
  actor engineer
  provides page:KitProject.app = james-habits-app
  when opens page:KitProject
  then sees heading:KitQuestionSheet
  then sees heading:KitNeverAdjudicated
  then sees button:KitAddBehaviour

behaviour BEH-PAIR-1 "A behaviour and the test Kit generates from it are on one screen"
  actor engineer
  provides page:KitBehaviour.app = james-habits-app
  provides page:KitBehaviour.id = BEH-SEED-1
  when opens page:KitBehaviour
  then sees heading:KitBehaviourSteps
  then sees heading:KitGeneratedTest
  contract the two are side by side, not on separate screens — the output must be in view
  contract the test is regenerated on every read, never cached

behaviour BEH-ADJ-1 "An inference can be adjudicated from the browser"
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitBehaviour
  then sees heading:KitAdjudication
  then sees button:KitApprove
  when activates button:KitApprove
  contract the corpus file on disk gains `review approved` for this behaviour
  contract the existing `review unreviewed` line is REPLACED, not joined by a second
  contract nothing is committed to git

behaviour BEH-ADJ-2 "Denying an inference asks for the correction it must carry"
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitBehaviour
  then sees button:KitDeny
  when fills field:KitCorrection with "the starter set is re-seeded whenever the list is empty"
  when activates button:KitDeny
  contract Deny is disabled while the correction is empty
  contract a denial with no correction is refused by the parser, and its sentence reaches the screen

behaviour BEH-STEP-1 "A step can be added while the generated test is in view"
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitBehaviour
  then sees heading:KitAddAStep
  then sees button:KitAddStep
  contract the page re-reads after the write, so the REGENERATED test is what is on screen

# ═══ Free-text notes (kit#118) ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
# "a less structured input in the ui where you can just comment on something
# rather than defining a behaviour and this follows the workflow to get folded
# into the spec and code" — and, 2026-10-10: "I want to be using kit but it just
# isn't quite natural yet". A note is NOT a behaviour: nothing parses it, and
# nothing generates from it. It is an inbox the engineer empties into the spec.
# Built in the same stack as this spec; the shape is his to change (kit#118, default 10-11).

behaviour BEH-NOTE-1 "A note can be left on a project without writing a behaviour"
  source defined kit#118
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitProject
  when fills field:KitNote with "the habit list should remember the order I dragged it into"
  when activates button:KitLeaveNote
  then sees region:KitOpenNotes
  contract the note is appended to <app>.notes.md beside the corpus, never into the .beh
  contract it is committed and pushed like any other edit, and the write result says so
  contract Leave note is disabled while the box is empty

behaviour BEH-NOTE-2 "A note left on a behaviour names that behaviour"
  source defined kit#118
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitBehaviour
  when fills field:KitNote with "this should also cover the empty list"
  when activates button:KitLeaveNote
  contract the note carries the behaviour's id, so it is listed under the project too
  contract the note survives the behaviour being renamed or removed — it is text, not a link

behaviour BEH-NOTE-3 "A note is removed in the change that folds it into the spec"
  source defined kit#118
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitProject
  then sees region:KitOpenNotes
  contract <app>.notes.md holds only notes nobody has folded yet; a folded note is deleted,
  contract not ticked, in the same pull request that adds the behaviour or code it became
  contract with no open notes the region says so, rather than vanishing
